---
import WorkLayout from '../../layouts/WorkLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';

const description = "Strive's chat agent renders answers as live, interactive UI. Mariappan Subramanian owns the frontend infrastructure behind it: a from-scratch streaming engine, a block and slot vocabulary, and the design system every agent team builds against.";
---
<WorkLayout
  title="Strive — generative UI chat"
  description={description}
  ogDescription="Strive's chat agent renders answers as live, interactive UI. The frontend infrastructure behind it: a from-scratch streaming engine, a block and slot vocabulary, and the design system every agent team builds against."
  eyebrow="Strive Labs · 2024 → now"
  heading="Strive — generative UI chat"
  facts={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Design engineer, frontend owner' },
    { label: 'Scope', value: '~85% of commits on the module' },
    { label: 'Status', value: 'In production, still shipping' },
  ]}
  link={{ href: 'https://strivelabs.ai/', label: 'strivelabs.ai' }}
  schema={{
    name: 'Strive — generative UI chat',
    url: 'https://strivelabs.ai/',
    description: 'The chat surface every Strive agent reaches the user through: a from-scratch streaming engine, a block and slot vocabulary, and rich generative interfaces rendered live.',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    keywords: 'generative UI, AI chat, streaming, React 19, TypeScript, design systems, design engineering',
  }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">A marketing OS that answers in <mark class="hl">live interfaces, not paragraphs</mark>. I own the frontend infrastructure behind it — streaming engine, block vocabulary, design system, the lot.</Fragment>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Strive's core product <em>is</em> the chat. The agent knows a customer's marketing context in and out, and replies in rich generative UI — charts, tables, forms, whole interactive surfaces.</li>
    <li>Nothing off the shelf. Data model, interface contract, streaming, validation layer — all built from scratch.</li>
    <li>One component, one streaming contract, one block vocabulary. Every agent team in the company writes against it instead of building their own UI.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The vocabulary I designed</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li><strong>Blocks</strong> — the singular unit. Interactive controls, presentation elements, rich charts, tables. The model composes an answer out of them.</li>
    <li><strong>Slots</strong> — a canvas beside the thread for things too big to inline: artifacts, editors, full agent interfaces that behave like standalone products. We shipped this before the industry settled on calling it a canvas or artifact panel.</li>
    <li><strong>Contracts</strong> — every streamed block is Zod-validated at the boundary, so a confused model degrades gracefully instead of blanking the screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worth calling out</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Block-by-block streaming — the UI assembles itself as the model thinks.</li>
    <li>A composer that does real work: file attachments, slash commands, skills, response modes, dictation.</li>
    <li>Multiple concurrent conversations with centralised abort control.</li>
    <li>Human-in-the-loop tools — the agent can stop and ask before it acts.</li>
    <li>Reasoning traces, agent presence, edit-and-resend, grounding citations with source favicons, a conversation minimap, next-step suggestions after every turn.</li>
    <li>Sidebar history with search, date bucketing, infinite scroll, rename and delete; in-thread pagination that loads older messages on scroll-to-top without losing scroll position.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The rebuild that fixed reliability</h2>
  <p>The first design asked the model for one big JSON payload holding every block. It worked until answers got complex — several charts, tables, interactive elements — and then it didn't: malformed JSON, truncated responses, silent retry loops.</p>
  <!-- .flow is white-space: pre, so the line breaks below are the layout. -->
  <div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="Before: the model returns a single JSON blob that is parsed into progressive blocks. After: the model emits individual tool calls, each parsed on its own, with plain text streaming alongside.">
<b class="bad">BEFORE</b>
LLM ─▶ one JSON blob ─▶ CONTENT_STREAM ─▶ parser ─▶ blocks

<b class="good">AFTER</b>
LLM ─▶ individual tool calls ─▶ TOOL_STREAM_START/CONTENT
    ─▶ per-tool parsing ─▶ blocks
    (plain text keeps streaming alongside)</div>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Each tool call parses on its own, so one bad block can't take the answer down with it.</li>
    <li>Smaller units mean the model stops trying to hold a whole document in its head — fewer truncations, fewer retries.</li>
    <li>Complexity became additive: richer answers just mean more tool calls, not a bigger blob.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it shows up</h2>
  <p>Mounted at the product's home route, embedded as a side panel in agent workspaces, and shipped as a sheet inside individual agent screens. Routines get created in it, reports get built in it, agent output gets reviewed and acted on in it.</p>

  <h2>Under the hood</h2>
  <dl class="kv">
    <div><dt>Core</dt><dd>React 19, TypeScript, Vite 6</dd></div>
    <div><dt>State</dt><dd>Zustand, TanStack Query, Context for live sessions</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Streaming</dt><dd>Fetch + SSE, custom incremental JSON parser</dd></div>
    <div><dt>UI</dt><dd>Tailwind, Radix, in-house <code>ui-core</code> / <code>ui-blocks</code>, Storybook</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Rendering</dt><dd>streamdown + react-markdown, Recharts, TanStack Table</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Validation</dt><dd>Zod schemas on every streamed block</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Models</dt><dd>Claude and OpenAI, configurable per deployment</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Backend</dt><dd>Fastify (Node)</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Platform</dt><dd>Clerk auth, PostHog analytics</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Testing</dt><dd>Vitest, Testing Library, MSW, Playwright E2E</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Design system</dt><dd>Storybook, an internal doc site, and Figma, kept in sync</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <!--
    Adding media: drop files into public/images/work/strive-gen-ui-chat/ and use one of these.

    <figure>
      <img src="/images/work/strive-gen-ui-chat/slot-canvas.png" alt="Describe what the image shows" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Slot canvas, opened beside a thread</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <video src="/images/work/strive-gen-ui-chat/streaming.mp4" poster="/images/work/strive-gen-ui-chat/streaming-poster.jpg" controls playsinline preload="none" width="1600" height="900"></video>
      <figcaption>Blocks streaming in, one at a time</figcaption>
    </figure>
  -->

  <blockquote>Chat is the easy part. The contract underneath it is the product.</blockquote>

  <aside class="next-up callout wobbly wobbly--2" aria-label="Upcoming write-up">
    <Icon name="pen" />
    <div>
      <strong>Deep dive: streaming generative UI, block by block</strong>
      <p>Parser internals, the tool-call migration, and everything that broke on the way. <span class="soon">Blog landing here soon.</span></p>
    </div>
  </aside>
</WorkLayout>
